WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
React Native revolutionized mobile development

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.920
by letting engineering teams write one JavaScript code base

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.920
and ship it to multiple platforms.

4
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.880
But for years, those apps faced a performance ceiling,

5
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.280
built into the frameworks core design.

6
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.960
The problem was structural.

7
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.360
The JavaScript code holding your apps logic

8
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.520
and the native device rendering the pixels

9
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:22.240
existed in entirely separate environments.

10
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:23.840
They could not communicate directly

11
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:25.440
across the gap between them.

12
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.800
To bridge that divide,

13
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.280
every single command had to be serialized.

14
00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:33.760
If a user tapped a button, that action was converted into a text-based JSON string,

15
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.280
so it could be sent across a mechanism literally called the bridge.

16
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.680
Once the JSON string made it across the bridge,

17
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.640
the native side had to asynchronously unpack that text

18
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.760
and deserialize it back into an actionable command.

19
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.720
Only then, with the device actually do what you asked.

20
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.800
This constant translation process required a massive amount of overhead.

21
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.920
No matter how clean your JavaScript code was,

22
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.160
the app could only run as fast as the bridge could process those incoming

23
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.280
and outgoing messages.

24
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.680
Because the bridge forced all communication to be asynchronous,

25
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.560
UI layout passes were calculated out of sync

26
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.240
with the actual drawing of the screen.

27
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.440
The JavaScript thread was sending dimensions,

28
00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:16.480
but the native thread was often already painting pixels.

29
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.640
For developers, this meant dealing with unpredictable UI flicker.

30
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.200
You couldn't get accurate measurements of an element

31
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.640
before the screen painted it, resulting in visual jumps

32
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.440
as components quickly resize themselves after the fact.

33
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.360
The architecture also forced a severe startup penalty.

34
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.120
Native modules, like the camera or the file system,

35
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:38.560
were eagerly initialized.

36
00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.560
The app loaded every single module into memory at startup,

37
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.720
whether the user needed them right then or not.

38
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.600
These issues were baked into the foundation.

39
00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.440
To support modern features like concurrent React,

40
00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:55.200
the core engineering team realized the bridge itself had to go.

41
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.880
They had to tear out the infrastructure and rebuild the system from scratch.

42
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.800
That rebuild is now the default engine in React Native version 0.76 and beyond.

43
00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.760
At the center of this new architecture is the foundational replacement for the bridge,

44
00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.080
the JavaScript interface, or JSI.

45
00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.360
Instead of translating commands into text strings,

46
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.200
JSI allows JavaScript to hold direct synchronous C++ references to native objects.

47
00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.280
This enables shared memory.

48
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.200
The two environments no longer pass serialized messages back and forth,

49
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.040
because they operate within the same space.

50
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.960
You can see the result in libraries like Reanimated 3.

51
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.680
It uses JSI workloads to run animation code directly on the UI thread,

52
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.480
hitting a flawless 120 frames per second,

53
00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.800
with zero round trips back to the JavaScript thread.

54
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:49.040
JSI transformed a delayed asynchronous conversation into instant direct device control.

55
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.600
With the communication layer fixed,

56
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.200
the team completely rewrote the UI rendering pipeline.

57
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.960
They built a new shared C++ renderer called Fabric,

58
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.240
designed specifically to work across iOS, Android,

59
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.000
and eventually desktop environments.

60
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.320
Because Fabric sits on top of the synchronous JSI connection,

61
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.040
it can measure and commit layout synchronously.

62
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:14.400
The screen only paints when the dimensions are perfectly calculated,

63
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.880
which ends the UI flicker of the old architecture.

64
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.920
Fabric also runs an automated performance optimization called view flattening.

65
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.440
If you have redundant layers of nested UI views in your code,

66
00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.160
Fabric detects them and strips them out before rendering,

67
00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.840
leaving a single optimized layer.

68
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.720
This synchronous nature means react native can finally utilize modern concurrent

69
00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:40.160
react features. Developers can now use use transition to mark state updates as non-urgent,

70
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.240
or suspense for complex data fetching.

71
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.520
Fabric's role extends beyond rendering speed.

72
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.560
By stabilizing the timing of layout and paint,

73
00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.920
it provides the reliability required for the most complex state management and UI interactions.

74
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.840
To fix the severe startup lag of the old architecture,

75
00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.720
the core team addressed the eager initialization problem by introducing turbo modules.

76
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.640
This timeline chart compares app boot processes to show how turbo modules utilize lazy loading.

77
00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.320
Instead of loading every native module at time zero,

78
00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.200
a turbo module only initializes at the exact millisecond a user actually accesses it.

79
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:20.880
These modules are also heavily optimized for safety.

80
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.040
They are backed by a tool called code gen,

81
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.000
which reads your TypeScript specifications and automatically generates the exact C++

82
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:31.600
glue code required, eliminating the need to write those connectors manually.

83
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.720
The final layer of optimization is Hermes,

84
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:38.880
Meta's custom built JavaScript engine designed specifically for react native.

85
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.720
Traditional engines compile JavaScript at runtime on the user's device.

86
00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:46.960
Hermes uses a head of time or AOT compilation.

87
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.960
It translates your JavaScript into dense bytecode during the build process

88
00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.560
before the app is ever downloaded.

89
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.240
When you combine the lazy loading of turbo modules with the ahead of time compilation of Hermes,

90
00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.920
the time it takes for an app to become interactive drops significantly

91
00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:03.600
and the total memory footprint shrinks.

92
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.640
The entire platform has moved away from being async, eager, and bridged,

93
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.520
and is now firmly synchronous, lazy, and direct.

94
00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.720
By rewriting the core communication and rendering pipelines in shared C++,

95
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.520
react native stripped away the translation overhead that previously limited cross-platform apps.

96
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.960
Developers can now write complex React logic while the device executes commands with native performance parity.

97
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.720
This is no longer an experiment or an opt-in feature.

98
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.120
As a version 0.76, the new architecture is on by default.

99
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.440
It is the reality of how your apps run.

100
00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:43.280
Understanding how these underlying synchronous systems operate is no longer optional.

101
00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:49.520
It is a mandatory requirement for engineering the next generation of high-performance mobile software.

